WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.728 --> 00:00:17.672
In a monk described a frightening attack on a monastery in England.

2
00:00:18.772 --> 00:00:21.553
Suddenly giant men jumped from the long ships.

3
00:00:22.653 --> 00:00:26.475
They were a head taller than any of us, and some had bright red hair.

4
00:00:27.515 --> 00:00:32.844
They had all manner of armor and weapons, and they killed every monk they saw, even though we

5
00:00:32.884 --> 00:00:33.784
had no weapons.

6
00:00:34.865 --> 00:00:36.125
Luckily I escaped.

7
00:00:36.825 --> 00:00:42.407
When I returned to the monastery, anything made of silver or gold was gone— everything of value.

8
00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:45.428
Who were these strange, terrifying warriors?

9
00:00:46.588 --> 00:00:47.968
The warriors were Vikings.

10
00:00:49.089 --> 00:00:54.070
From the eighth to the eleventh century, the Vikings raided the coasts of Europe during an era now

11
00:00:54.150 --> 00:00:55.230
called the Viking Age.

12
00:00:56.611 --> 00:01:00.512
England, France, Scotland, and Ireland were prey to their attacks.

13
00:01:01.568 --> 00:01:07.733
The Vikings came from the Scandinavian countries in northern Europe now known as Norway, Sweden, and Denmark.

14
00:01:08.714 --> 00:01:12.276
They were also called Norsemen, which means men from the north.

15
00:01:13.517 --> 00:01:20.222
Viking warriors stole whatever they found and killed many people, usually attacking with iron swords and large battle-axes.

16
00:01:21.283 --> 00:01:26.987
They wore skillfully made metal armor, and for defense, they carried round wooden shields with a center made

17
00:01:27.028 --> 00:01:27.368
of iron.

18
00:01:28.352 --> 00:01:34.095
Today many pictures show Vikings wearing helmets with horns, but this idea is an error that began in

19
00:01:34.136 --> 00:01:35.176
the nineteenth century.

20
00:01:36.197 --> 00:01:39.138
Most Viking warriors actually wore simple iron caps.

21
00:01:40.439 --> 00:01:42.220
At home the Vikings were farmers.

22
00:01:43.261 --> 00:01:49.825
They kept cattle, pigs, ducks, and chickens, and their farmlands produced crops like wheat and rye, as well

23
00:01:49.865 --> 00:01:50.505
as vegetables.

24
00:01:51.626 --> 00:01:55.028
Early spring was a busy time as they prepared their crops for planting.

25
00:01:56.020 --> 00:02:01.142
With help from slaves and servants, they plowed and fertilized the fields and planted seeds for grains.

26
00:02:02.203 --> 00:02:07.105
And then, after this spring work was finished, the Vikings left to attack foreign lands.

27
00:02:08.505 --> 00:02:13.467
The Vikings set sail in their boats to raid monasteries and villages for precious metals and for people

28
00:02:13.507 --> 00:02:14.128
to enslave.

29
00:02:15.228 --> 00:02:19.970
Carved from wood, their "longships" enabled the Vikings to travel swiftly and efficiently.

30
00:02:21.050 --> 00:02:24.472
The ships were light and long, with high curves at the front and back.

31
00:02:25.568 --> 00:02:31.071
They had oars along their entire length and a square sail above, so the Vikings could travel on

32
00:02:31.111 --> 00:02:33.792
the sea and then use the oars to make a speedy attack.

33
00:02:35.033 --> 00:02:39.295
Many of the ships also had a tall, fierce-looking dragon carved into their prows.

34
00:02:40.396 --> 00:02:44.198
When villagers saw one of these ships approaching, they often fled in terror!

35
00:02:45.178 --> 00:02:49.561
Because their boats were shallow, the Vikings were also able to travel inland by river.

36
00:02:50.501 --> 00:02:52.522
Their trade routes reached deep into Europe.

37
00:02:53.563 --> 00:02:58.205
France, Poland, and Russia were all part of the dynamic world of Viking commerce.

38
00:02:59.566 --> 00:03:05.488
Eventually the Vikings realized that many of the countries they raided (or traded with) were better for farming

39
00:03:05.588 --> 00:03:06.569
than their own homeland.

40
00:03:07.629 --> 00:03:13.612
They began to establish forts or settlements, and from these strongholds, they continued their raids and conquered new

41
00:03:13.672 --> 00:03:20.415
territory. The Vikings' ships were small, but they were very sturdy, which allowed the Vikings to travel to

42
00:03:20.455 --> 00:03:22.796
faraway places and become great explorers.

43
00:03:23.896 --> 00:03:25.597
Iceland was first settled by Vikings.

44
00:03:26.678 --> 00:03:31.540
When the famous Viking Erik the Red was banished from Iceland, he set sail in his longship in

45
00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:32.441
search of adventure.

46
00:03:33.481 --> 00:03:38.984
He discovered Greenland, and one of his sons, Leif Erikson, was the first European to reach North America.

47
00:03:39.985 --> 00:03:46.549
Leif sailed west of Greenland and discovered Baffin Island, Labrador, and Newfoundland almost five hundred years before Columbus

48
00:03:46.609 --> 00:03:47.309
reached America.

49
00:03:48.490 --> 00:03:54.505
Strangely enough, despite their warlike manner, the Vikings often adapted to, and joined the people they conquered.

50
00:03:55.566 --> 00:04:01.770
Over time the Vikings who conquered England became English; the Vikings who conquered Normandy quickly became part of

51
00:04:01.830 --> 00:04:03.591
its native French-speaking population.

52
00:04:04.572 --> 00:04:08.255
While the Vikings were raiders and pirates, they were also traders and merchants.

53
00:04:09.315 --> 00:04:13.038
Their trading ships carried fur, ivory, amber, and slaves.

54
00:04:14.179 --> 00:04:18.402
The Vikings were superb metalworkers, so they exported swords and other weapons.

55
00:04:19.523 --> 00:04:21.204
And some Vikings worked as mercenaries.

56
00:04:22.188 --> 00:04:26.552
They were never a unified group; instead they were separate bands of sea pirates.

57
00:04:27.653 --> 00:04:33.258
The Vikings are often portrayed as barbaric savages, but they were well organized and had a written language

58
00:04:33.298 --> 00:04:33.918
called Runic.

59
00:04:36.320 --> 00:04:41.445
The Vikings' mastery of this written language helped them conquer many opponents because it allowed them to send

60
00:04:41.485 --> 00:04:42.366
messages with ease.

61
00:04:43.527 --> 00:04:48.751
Much of their heritage survives today in the Norse sagas, which describe the adventures of their heroes and

62
00:04:48.791 --> 00:04:55.946
gods. These great legends inspired famous operas and poems, written many centuries after the Viking Age ended.

63
00:04:57.147 --> 00:04:59.268
The Viking heritage is still remembered today.

64
00:05:00.328 --> 00:05:06.511
In Scandinavia there are several Viking ship museums, where people can see restored Viking ships and admire the

65
00:05:06.551 --> 00:05:08.872
amazing craftsmanship of these bold sailors.

66
00:05:09.992 --> 00:05:12.333
And many cities hold annual Viking festivals.

67
00:05:13.234 --> 00:05:18.176
People can live and dress as Vikings, and craft weapons and jewelry using traditional Viking methods.

68
00:05:19.428 --> 00:05:22.129
Some people even participate in mock Viking battles!

69
00:05:23.229 --> 00:05:28.711
During the Viking age, the "men from the north" explored distant lands—and terrorized many people.

70
00:05:29.771 --> 00:05:35.473
With their longships and thirst for adventure, they left a permanent mark on both ship technology and European

71
00:05:35.533 --> 00:05:35.914
history.
